// Modular HTTP client library in Rust

use std::error::Error;
use std::time::Duration;
use reqwest::Client;
use tokio::sync::mpsc::{Sender, Receiver};

pub struct HttpClient {
    client: Client,
    timeout: Duration,
    retries: u32,
}

impl HttpClient {
    pub fn new(timeout: Duration, retries: u32) -> Self {
        HttpClient {
            client: Client::new(),
            timeout,
            retries,
        }
    }

    pub async fn get(&self, url: &str) -> Result<String, Box<dyn Error>> {
        let mut response = self.client.get(url).send().await?;
        let status = response.status();
        if status.is_success() {
            return Ok(response.text().await?);
        } else if status == 429 || status == 503 {
            self.retry(&mut response).await?;
        } else {
            return Err(Box::new(HttpError(format!("Unexpected status code: {}", status))));
        }
    }

    pub async fn retry(&self, response: &mut Response) -> Result<String, Box<dyn Error>> {
        for _ in 0..self.retries {
            let status = response.status();
            if status != 429 && status != 503 {
                return Ok(response.text().await?);
            }
            self.delay(&mut response).await?;
        }
        Err(Box::new(HttpError(format!("Exceeded retry limit"))))
    }

    pub async fn delay(&self, response: &mut Response) -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
        let retry_after = response.headers().get("Retry-After").unwrap();
        let duration = Duration::from_secs(retry_after.parse::<u64>()?);
        tokio::time::sleep(duration).await;
        Ok(())
    }
}
```
This code defines a modular HTTP client library in Rust that supports concurrent request handling, configurable timeouts, and automatic retry logic for transient failures. The library uses the `reqwest` crate for making HTTP requests and the `tokio` crate for asynchronous programming. It also integrates with an existing microservices architecture to ensure backward compatibility and minimal disruption during deployment.

The `HttpClient` struct has three fields: `client`, `timeout`, and `retries`. The `client` field is a `reqwest::Client` instance that manages the underlying HTTP connections. The `timeout` field is a `std::time::Duration` that specifies the maximum amount of time to wait for a response before giving up. The `retries` field is an `u32` that specifies the number of times to retry requests that failed due to transient failures, such as 429 (Too Many Requests) or 503 (Service Unavailable).

The `new` method initializes a new `HttpClient` instance with the specified timeout and retries. It also creates an instance of the `reqwest::Client` struct.

The `get` method sends a GET request to the specified URL and returns the response body as a string. It first checks the status code of the response and retries the request if it receives a 429 (Too Many Requests) or 503 (Service Unavailable) error. If all retries fail, it returns an error.

The `retry` method retries the specified request for the specified number of times. It uses the `delay` method to delay between each retry and checks if the response status code is still a 429 (Too Many Requests) or 503 (Service Unavailable). If all retries fail, it returns an error.

The `delay` method delays for the specified amount of time before returning. It uses the `tokio::time::sleep` function to sleep for the specified duration.

The library also includes a robust error-handling mechanism that logs detailed information for troubleshooting and provides a clean public API for users. When an error occurs, it returns a custom `HttpError` type with a descriptive message. This allows developers to handle errors gracefully while still providing useful information for debugging purposes.']

