['Here is a sample Java service that processes a list of file paths provided via an HTTP endpoint, using caching to avoid redundant hashing of files and optimizing for both memory usage and speed:
```
import java.io.File;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;
import org.apache.commons.io.FileUtils;

public class FileHashService {
  private ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> hashCache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

  public void processFilePaths(List<String> filePaths) {
    for (String filePath : filePaths) {
      if (!hashCache.containsKey(filePath)) {
        try {
          File file = new File(filePath);
          if (file.exists()) {
            String hash = getFileHash(file);
            hashCache.put(filePath, hash);
          } else {
            // Handle non-existent files
          }
        } catch (Exception e) {
          // Handle errors
        }
      }
    }
  }

  private String getFileHash(File file) throws Exception {
    MessageDigest digester = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
    byte[] buffer = new byte[1024];
    try (InputStream in = new BufferedInputStream(new FileInputStream(file))) {
      int read;
      while ((read = in.read(buffer)) > 0) {
        digester.update(buffer, 0, read);
      }
    }
    return digester.digestAsHex();
  }
}
```
This service uses a `ConcurrentHashMap` to cache the file hashes, which allows for thread-safe operations and ensures that files are only hashed once even if multiple requests are processed concurrently. The caching mechanism avoids redundant hashing of files that have already been processed, reducing the overall processing time and memory usage.

To optimize performance, the service uses a buffered input stream to read the file data in 1024-byte chunks. This reduces the number of system calls made by the `FileInputStream` and improves overall performance.

The caching mechanism can be evaluated based on the trade-offs between memory usage and speed when managing cached file hashes. For example, if the cache is too small, it may not cover all the files in the dataset, leading to redundant hashing and increased processing time. On the other hand, if the cache is too large, it may consume excessive memory resources, reducing performance. A good starting point for the cache size would be to benchmark the service with different cache sizes and measure the trade-offs between memory usage and speed.

To scale horizontally, the service can be deployed on multiple instances, using a load balancer or a reverse proxy to distribute incoming requests across them. This allows the service to handle increased load without significant degradation in performance. The caching mechanism should also be designed to work well with multiple instances, with each instance having its own cache and handling only a subset of the files in the dataset.']

