    return 0;
}

int validateRecords(long *offset_array, int *key_array, double *float_array, int size) {
    // Validate the range of values in each array
    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        if (offset_array[i] < offset || key_array[i] < key_min || key_array[i] > key_max || float_array[i] < float_min || float_array[i] > float_max) {
            return 1;
        }
    }

    // Validate the uniqueness of values in each array
    for (int i = 0; i < size - 1; ++i) {
        if (offset_array[i] == offset_array[i + 1] || key_array[i] == key_array[i + 1] || float_array[i] == float_array[i + 1]) {
            return 1;
        }
    }

    // Validate the distribution of values in each array
    double total = 0.0;
    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        total += float_array[i];
    }

    if (total != (float_max - float_min) * size) {
        return 1;
    }

    return 0;
}
```
In this implementation, the `generateRecords` function takes five parameters: the offset value for each record, the minimum and maximum values for the integer and floating-point fields, and the size of the arrays. The function first allocates memory for the three arrays using `malloc`, and then initializes them with random values within specified ranges using a loop.

After initializing the arrays, the function calls the `validateRecords` function to validate the records against predefined criteria. If any record fails validation, the function frees all allocated memory and returns an appropriate error status. Otherwise, it integrates with an external data processing library to validate the records before returning them.

The `validateRecords` function validates the range of values in each array, the uniqueness of values in each array, and the distribution of values in each array. It does this by checking if any value falls outside the specified ranges for the integer and floating-point fields, or if there are any duplicate values in either array. If any of these conditions are met, the function returns an error status. Otherwise, it returns a success status.

This implementation balances between usability, maintainability, and scalability by providing a clear interface for generating random records with specified criteria, using efficient data structures to manage memory, and validating records against predefined criteria before returning them. It also considers the constrained environment where memory and processing power are limited, and optimizes the implementation for efficiency while ensuring robustness and reliability under varying load conditions.']

