### 4.2.1.3. Encode/Decode RB_ID
The RB_ID IE is used to identify the Radio Bearer for which the UE is requesting the QoS parameters. The RB_ID value ranges from 1 to 15, and it is encoded using a binary representation (i.e., each bit in the byte corresponds to one of the RB_ID values).
```c
// Encode RB_ID
uint8_t encodeRbId(int rbId) {
    uint8_t encodedVal = 0;

    // Check if the RB_ID is valid
    if (rbId < 1 || rbId > 15) {
        return -1;
    }

    // Calculate the binary representation of the RB_ID value
    while (rbId > 0) {
        encodedVal = (encodedVal << 1) | (rbId % 2);
        rbId /= 2;
    }

    return encodedVal;
}
```

### 4.2.1.4. Decode/Encode QFI
The QFI IE is used to specify the Quality of Service parameters for a specific Radio Bearer. The QFI value ranges from 1 to 9, and it is encoded using a binary representation (i.e., each bit in the byte corresponds to one of the QFI values).
```c
// Encode QFI
uint8_t encodeQfi(int qfi) {
    uint8_t encodedVal = 0;

    // Check if the QFI is valid
    if (qfi < 1 || qfi > 9) {
        return -1;
    }

    // Calculate the binary representation of the QFI value
    while (qfi > 0) {
        encodedVal = (encodedVal << 1) | (qfi % 2);
        qfi /= 2;
    }

    return encodedVal;
}
```

### 4.2.1.5. Calculate QFI value from RB_ID and CU_CP
The QFI value can be calculated by taking the value of RB_ID and multiplying it with the value of CU_CP. The result is then encoded using a binary representation as specified in Section 4.2.1.4.
```c
// Calculate QFI value from RB_ID and CU_CP
uint8_t calculateQfi(int rbId, int cuCp) {
    return encodeQfi((rbId * cuCp) % 9);
}
```

### 4.2.1.6. Calculate the minimum and maximum values of QFI
The minimum value of QFI is 0 (i.e., no Quality of Service parameters are required for any Radio Bearer), while the maximum value of QFI is 9 (i.e., the highest possible Quality of Service parameters are requested for all Radio Bearers). The calculation of these values can be done as follows:
```c
// Calculate minimum and maximum values of QFI
int minQfi = 0;
int maxQfi = 9;
```

### 4.2.1.7. Check if the QFI value is valid
The QFI value must be between the minimum and maximum values (i.e., between 0 and 9) in order for it to be considered a valid value. The check can be done as follows:
```c
// Check if the QFI value is valid
if (qfi < minQfi || qfi > maxQfi) {
    return -1;
}
