// Function to manage a flexible event processing system
void processEvents(int maxBufferSize, int priorityLevel) {
  // Circular buffer to store events
  struct Event {
    time_t timestamp;
    int priority;
    char* message;
  };
  std::vector<Event> events;

  // Function to insert an event into the circular buffer
  void insertEvent(const time_t& timestamp, const int& priority, const char* message) {
    // Check if the buffer is full and needs to be resized
    if (events.size() == maxBufferSize) {
      // Resize the buffer by half to make room for new events
      events.resize(maxBufferSize * 0.5);
    }

    // Create a new event structure with the given data
    Event event = {timestamp, priority, message};

    // Add the event to the circular buffer
    events.push_back(event);
  }

  // Function to log an event insertion
  void logEventInsertion() {
    // Log the event with a high-priority logger
    LOGGER::Log("Event inserted", LOGGER::Priority::High);
  }

  // Function to handle out-of-memory conditions
  void handleOutOfMemory() {
    // Throttle event generation by reducing the priority level
    priorityLevel = priorityLevel * 0.5;

    // Discard less critical events
    for (int i = 0; i < events.size(); ++i) {
      if (events[i].priority < priorityLevel) {
        events.erase(events.begin() + i);
        break;
      }
    }
  }

  // Function to adjust the buffer size based on operational needs
  void adjustBufferSize(int newSize) {
    maxBufferSize = newSize;
  }

  // Function to allow for seamless integration with existing infrastructure
  void integrateWithExistingInfrastructure() {
    // Implement a mechanism to handle out-of-memory conditions gracefully
    handleOutOfMemory();

    // Allow for configuration options to adjust the buffer size and logging behavior
    adjustBufferSize(maxBufferSize);
  }
}
```
This code block implements a function that manages a flexible event processing system. It includes a circular buffer of events, each of which has a timestamp, a priority level, and a message. The buffer is dynamically allocated and resized as needed to ensure efficient memory usage and prevent leaks. The system also supports logging mechanisms to record event insertions, allowing for monitoring and debugging.

The function `insertEvent` inserts an event into the circular buffer and logs the insertion with a high-priority logger. If the buffer is full and needs to be resized, it resizes the buffer by half and discards less critical events to make room for new events.

The function `handleOutOfMemory` handles out-of-memory conditions by reducing the priority level of event generation and discarding less critical events. This prevents the system from crashing or becoming unstable due to high-frequency event generation.

The function `adjustBufferSize` allows for configuration options to adjust the buffer size based on operational needs. This can be useful for scaling the system to support increased event volumes while maintaining consistent performance and reliability.

Finally, the function `integrateWithExistingInfrastructure` allows for seamless integration with existing infrastructure by implementing a mechanism to handle out-of-memory conditions gracefully and allowing for configuration options to adjust the buffer size and logging behavior. This can help minimize disruption during deployment and ensure that the system continues to function correctly even in high-traffic situations.']

