The memory management component uses `std::vector<ItemContainer*>` to store pointers to the containers that will hold the generated items. Each container has a `double`, a `string`, and a `std::vector<int>` field, which are initialized with random values within specified ranges. The memory is allocated using `new` and released using `delete`.

The task manager schedules tasks for each item in parallel, using the `async()` function from the `asyncio` library to create an asynchronous task that performs validation and synchronization with an external data processing library. If validation fails, the memory is released and retry is attempted. The tasks are joined using the `get()` method of the `future<void>` object returned by `async()`, which ensures that all tasks have completed before moving on to the next step.

