In this example, we have implemented a system that can dynamically allocate and initialize data structures based on the input from sensors. The system uses a combination of hash tables and linked lists to efficiently store and manage the data.

The `init` function is used to initialize the data structures with default values. The `process_new_data` function is used to handle concurrent access to shared resources. The `handle_new_phy_stats_id` and `handle_new_ue` functions are used to dynamically allocate and initialize data structures when a new PHY stats ID or UE is detected, respectively.

The system uses a combination of hash tables and linked lists to efficiently store and manage the data. The hash tables are used to quickly look up data based on its key, while the linked lists are used to maintain the order in which data was added to the system. This allows for efficient insertion and deletion of data, as well as quick access to specific data elements.

The system also uses a semaphore to manage concurrent access to shared resources. The `sem` variable is used to keep track of the number of items that are available in the queue. When new data is available, the system will acquire a read lock on the shared resources and then process the new data. Once processing is complete, the system will release the read lock and continue monitoring the queue for new data.

Overall, this example demonstrates how to use hash tables and linked lists in C to efficiently store and manage large amounts of data, while also managing concurrent access to shared resources using a semaphore.']

